            <h1 class="blog_content-title">Candace Owens vs. Andrew Wilson: Thoughts on the Debate</h1>
            <img src="images/politics/candace_owens.png" alt="Candace Owens" class="blog-scene">
            <h3 class="blog_content-date">August 15, 2026</h3>

            <p class="blog_content-p">I watched the Candace Owens vs. Andrew Wilson debate and wanted to share a few thoughts. The short version: I don't think either side clearly won on the actual topic of the debate. That topic was whether the evidence overwhelmingly shows Tyler Robinson was a patsy or that he committed the crimes he's accused of — Candace arguing patsy, Andrew arguing guilty.</p>

            <p class="blog_content-p">Andrew effectively argued that all the evidence directly tied to the case points overwhelmingly to Robinson's guilt. Candace, meanwhile, raised a number of details that cast doubt, and taken together, they could lead a reasonable person to suspect something larger is going on.</p>

            <p class="blog_content-p">Consider the screwdriver on the roof with Tyler's DNA on it, the gun found with ammunition belonging to Tyler and his prints on it, the text messages Tyler sent his boyfriend, video evidence of him on campus, and the fact that Tyler's own parents haven't claimed their son is being set up. Taken together, it's easy to conclude Tyler Robinson was responsible.</p>

            <p class="blog_content-p">Candace countered with a disputed restaurant receipt placing Tyler elsewhere, slowed-down video she suggested showed an exploding mic rather than a gunshot, questions about the surveillance footage, references to the French Foreign Legion and Israeli cell phones in the area, a TP USA backpack that was reportedly turned over rather than logged as evidence, texts showing Charlie growing more critical of Israel, and a handful of other loose threads.</p>

            <p class="blog_content-p">This is where I think Andrew won — though not on the debate's actual central question. If you're already inclined to think Tyler is a patsy, Candace gave you enough to keep that doubt alive. But a patsy requires someone else behind the assassination, and Candace has repeatedly implicated specific people and groups. Andrew pressed her on each claim, asking her to back it with evidence — and she couldn't.</p>

            <p class="blog_content-p">Candace said she believes France and Israel were behind the assassination. When Andrew asked for anything that directly links either country to the crime, she had nothing. The best she offered was Charlie's messages showing growing criticism of Israel after October 7th, and the presence of Israeli phones and French Foreign Legion personnel in the area. Even granting all of that as fact, none of it proves involvement — it only carries weight if you already hold beliefs about what these countries are capable of, shaped by other, unrelated history. In the end, Candace had to concede it comes down to intuition, not evidence — that more investigation is needed and she's "just asking questions."</p>

            <p class="blog_content-p">Her other major claim, that Erika Kirk and TP USA were somehow involved, collapsed just as fast. She backed off almost immediately, saying she didn't want legal trouble and clarifying she only meant Erika was complicit in pushing the "Tyler did it" narrative — not in planning anything. Every group she'd directly named, she walked back under pressure, after having spent considerable airtime casting doubt on their character.</p>

            <p class="blog_content-p">That's the moment Andrew landed his strongest blow. Anyone who has accused Candace of fabricating claims now has her on record retreating from nearly all of them — clips that will circulate on podcasts for weeks, chipping away at her credibility, and that leave her exposed to further scrutiny from TP USA and Erika Kirk if she tries to walk any of it back again.</p>

            <p class="blog_content-p">Maybe Tyler Robinson is a patsy. Maybe someone else was behind Charlie Kirk's assassination. But guilt isn't decided by what feels plausible — it's decided by what can be proven in a court of law. And right now, the only evidence that actually connects to this case points to one conclusion: Tyler Robinson shot Charlie Kirk.</p>

